      "text": "Database Indexes",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "Ek14g9OGWEjZl4FaZ7M85",
      "originalText": "Database Indexes",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "PmsLHZRf8Z9PbFPP2LOGZ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -198.60003662109375,
      "y": 1361,
      "width": 862.7993774414062,
      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ah",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 14308072,
      "version": 1730,
      "versionNonce": 300180376,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781034535788,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- What is an index:- \n      It is a seperate data structure which we maintain along side\n       our main data store (like tables for rdbms) which can help \n       us to improve our searches\n\n- Consider an array we can only do linear search in array now to optimize the search\nwhenever we add data in an array we also maintain the same data in the balanced binary\nsearch tree and during the search we search in the bbst not the array\n \n- n (no of items)\n    n = 1000 -> linear search -> approx 1000 checks -> BBST -> approx 10 checks\n    n = 10^6 -> linear search -> approx 10^6 checks -> BBST -> approx 20 checks\n    n = 10^9 -> linear search -> approx 10^9 checks -> BBST -> approx 30 checks\n\n- problems: 1. wastage of space (copy of data is maintained at 2 places)\n              2. Insertion takes hit (logn)\n\n- Full table scan -> linear search -> without indexes \n\n- Explain Selct * from products where price > 65000; \n           - this will give that table scan in happening or not\n\n- CREATE INDEX idx_price ON products (price); - Query to create index in MQSQL\n- Explain Selct * from products where price > 65000; \n          - after creating index and we execute the same query it will say something like\n            range search happening\n- ALTER TABLE products DROP INDEX idx_price; - delete an index\n\n                                                     \n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- What is an index:- \n      It is a seperate data structure which we maintain along side\n       our main data store (like tables for rdbms) which can help \n       us to improve our searches\n\n- Consider an array we can only do linear search in array now to optimize the search\nwhenever we add data in an array we also maintain the same data in the balanced binary\nsearch tree and during the search we search in the bbst not the array\n \n- n (no of items)\n    n = 1000 -> linear search -> approx 1000 checks -> BBST -> approx 10 checks\n    n = 10^6 -> linear search -> approx 10^6 checks -> BBST -> approx 20 checks\n    n = 10^9 -> linear search -> approx 10^9 checks -> BBST -> approx 30 checks\n\n- problems: 1. wastage of space (copy of data is maintained at 2 places)\n              2. Insertion takes hit (logn)\n\n- Full table scan -> linear search -> without indexes \n\n- Explain Selct * from products where price > 65000; \n           - this will give that table scan in happening or not\n\n- CREATE INDEX idx_price ON products (price); - Query to create index in MQSQL\n- Explain Selct * from products where price > 65000; \n          - after creating index and we execute the same query it will say something like\n            range search happening\n- ALTER TABLE products DROP INDEX idx_price; - delete an index\n\n                                                     \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "hSOwpndr6otmcSSNZYHR5",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -296,
      "y": 2109.7999877929688,
      "width": 934.17919921875,
      "height": 200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ai",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 281560728,
      "version": 439,
      "versionNonce": 129173144,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781031737183,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Database stores the data in the disk not the ram \n   - Disk space goes towards full capacity -> system performance decreases\n  \n- Virtual memory :- process in ram maintains a process state keeps the copy of that in harddisk\n  so when the disk is full the system cannot accquire enough virtual memory\n\n- Disk fragementation :- \n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Database stores the data in the disk not the ram \n   - Disk space goes towards full capacity -> system performance decreases\n  \n- Virtual memory :- process in ram maintains a process state keeps the copy of that in harddisk\n  so when the disk is full the system cannot accquire enough virtual memory\n\n- Disk fragementation :- \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ZeieFszSLRnqut1Ck10Dp",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -289.6000061035156,
      "y": 2390.199981689453,
      "width": 1125.6392822265625,
      "height": 700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ak",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1226440936,
      "version": 2367,
      "versionNonce": 898101400,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781034193905,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-> How these indexes internally looks like \n    - Tree based indexes :- underlying datastructure is a trees, generally the most common trees\n                                  are B-Trees (modified m-way trees), LSM Trees (most common in nosql)\n    - hash based indexes :- works on the bases on hashing algorithm a set of columns are stores with\n                                 a hash value in hash stores\n\n- Composite index\n     - Indexes with more than one columns\n     - CREATE INDEX idx_price_rating_category on products (price, rating, category_id); \n     - composite index (a,b,c,d) \n           - follows leftmost prefix rule -> index search is triggered if the columns involved are \n                 a valid prefix\n           - (created_at, price, rating, category_id)\n           - valid prefix : created_at, created_at, price, created_at, price, rating, \n                   created_at, price,rating,category_di\n           - in modern data bases, out of order prefix is automatically handled\n           - if prefixes are not being used full table scan is being done\n           - select * from products where created_at = now() and rating=3; // not a prefix\n                  - why? index hits even if no prefix indexes\n             \n          -  select * from products where created_at = '{string:time}' and rating=3; \n                  - this query fails to apply index because index of created_at is being created on timestamp and\n                      we are trying to fetch data in string base\n          - Query planner also do some optimization besides indexing, ICP(Index Condition) optimization\n          - (a, c) :- it will filter on 'a' as 'a' is a valid prefix, and then the filtered result will be checked for condition\n                       on 'c'\n\n ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-> How these indexes internally looks like \n    - Tree based indexes :- underlying datastructure is a trees, generally the most common trees\n                                  are B-Trees (modified m-way trees), LSM Trees (most common in nosql)\n    - hash based indexes :- works on the bases on hashing algorithm a set of columns are stores with\n                                 a hash value in hash stores\n\n- Composite index\n     - Indexes with more than one columns\n     - CREATE INDEX idx_price_rating_category on products (price, rating, category_id); \n     - composite index (a,b,c,d) \n           - follows leftmost prefix rule -> index search is triggered if the columns involved are \n                 a valid prefix\n           - (created_at, price, rating, category_id)\n           - valid prefix : created_at, created_at, price, created_at, price, rating, \n                   created_at, price,rating,category_di\n           - in modern data bases, out of order prefix is automatically handled\n           - if prefixes are not being used full table scan is being done\n           - select * from products where created_at = now() and rating=3; // not a prefix\n                  - why? index hits even if no prefix indexes\n             \n          -  select * from products where created_at = '{string:time}' and rating=3; \n                  - this query fails to apply index because index of created_at is being created on timestamp and\n                      we are trying to fetch data in string base\n          - Query planner also do some optimization besides indexing, ICP(Index Condition) optimization\n          - (a, c) :- it will filter on 'a' as 'a' is a valid prefix, and then the filtered result will be checked for condition\n                       on 'c'\n\n ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "HCQX0MjT0mWBwU1sKtl_7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -165.5999755859375,
      "y": 3130.9999771118164,
      "width": 1304.0391845703125,
      "height": 1825,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "al",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1711642520,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 2122748312,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781034478576,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "DROP DATABASE fakecommerce;\n\nCREATE DATABASE fakecommerce;\n\nuse fakecommerce;\n\nshow tables;\n\ndesc products;\ndesc categories;\ndesc orders;\ndesc order_products;\ndesc reviews;\n\nselect * from flyway_schema_history;\nselect * from products;\nselect * from categories;\nselect * from orders;\nselect * from order_products;\n\nselect count(*) from products where price > 80; -- 301\nEXPLAIN select * from products where price > 80; -- index scan\nEXPLAIN select * from products where created_at = NOW() and price > 80; -- index\nEXPLAIN select * from products where price > 80 and created_at = NOW(); -- index\nEXPLAIN select * from products where price = 80 and rating = 3; -- full table scan\nEXPLAIN select * from products where rating = 3 and  price = 80 ; -- full table scan\n\nEXPLAIN select * from products where created_at = NOW() and rating = 3 ; -- not a prefix , \nexplain select * from products where created_at <= NOW() ;  -- 1002\nselect count(*) from products where created_at <= CONVERT_TZ('2026-03-21 22:23:29', '+05:30', @@session.time_zone);  -- 1002\n\nexplain select * from products where created_at <= '2026-03-21 22:30:29' ; -- 1002\nexplain select * from products where created_at <= CONVERT_TZ('2026-03-21 22:23:29', '+05:30', @@session.time_zone);  -- index\n\n\n\nexplain select * from products where price = 80  and rating > 3 ; \n\nINSERT INTO products (title, description, price, category_id, image, rating, created_at)\nWITH RECURSIVE seq AS (\n    SELECT 1 AS n\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT n + 1 FROM seq WHERE n < 1000\n)\nSELECT \n    CONCAT('Awesome Product ', n),                                 \n    CONCAT('This is an automatically generated description for product #', n),\n    ROUND((RAND() * 100) + 10, 2),                                 \n    1,                                         \n    CONCAT('https://example.com/images/prod_', n, '.jpg'),         \n    ROUND((RAND() * 4) + 1, 1),                                    -- FIX: Now generates a pure number like 4.2\n    NOW(6)                                                         \nFROM seq;\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_price ON products (price); -- create an index\nALTER TABLE products DROP INDEX idx_price_rating_category; -- delete an index\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_price_rating_category ON products (created_at, price, rating, category_id); -- create an index\n\nSHOW INDEX FROM products;\n\n\n\n\nSELECT p.*, c.name FROM products p INNER JOIN categories c on p.category_id = c.id where p.id = 1;\n\nSELECT DISTINCT category FROM products;\n\nselect * from flyway_schema_history;\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_price ON products (price) ALGORITHM=INPLACE LOCK=NONE;\nALTER TABLE products DROP INDEX idx_price;\nEXPLAIN select * from products where price > 65000;",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "DROP DATABASE fakecommerce;\n\nCREATE DATABASE fakecommerce;\n\nuse fakecommerce;\n\nshow tables;\n\ndesc products;\ndesc categories;\ndesc orders;\ndesc order_products;\ndesc reviews;\n\nselect * from flyway_schema_history;\nselect * from products;\nselect * from categories;\nselect * from orders;\nselect * from order_products;\n\nselect count(*) from products where price > 80; -- 301\nEXPLAIN select * from products where price > 80; -- index scan\nEXPLAIN select * from products where created_at = NOW() and price > 80; -- index\nEXPLAIN select * from products where price > 80 and created_at = NOW(); -- index\nEXPLAIN select * from products where price = 80 and rating = 3; -- full table scan\nEXPLAIN select * from products where rating = 3 and  price = 80 ; -- full table scan\n\nEXPLAIN select * from products where created_at = NOW() and rating = 3 ; -- not a prefix , \nexplain select * from products where created_at <= NOW() ;  -- 1002\nselect count(*) from products where created_at <= CONVERT_TZ('2026-03-21 22:23:29', '+05:30', @@session.time_zone);  -- 1002\n\nexplain select * from products where created_at <= '2026-03-21 22:30:29' ; -- 1002\nexplain select * from products where created_at <= CONVERT_TZ('2026-03-21 22:23:29', '+05:30', @@session.time_zone);  -- index\n\n\n\nexplain select * from products where price = 80  and rating > 3 ; \n\nINSERT INTO products (title, description, price, category_id, image, rating, created_at)\nWITH RECURSIVE seq AS (\n    SELECT 1 AS n\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT n + 1 FROM seq WHERE n < 1000\n)\nSELECT \n    CONCAT('Awesome Product ', n),                                 \n    CONCAT('This is an automatically generated description for product #', n),\n    ROUND((RAND() * 100) + 10, 2),                                 \n    1,                                         \n    CONCAT('https://example.com/images/prod_', n, '.jpg'),         \n    ROUND((RAND() * 4) + 1, 1),                                    -- FIX: Now generates a pure number like 4.2\n    NOW(6)                                                         \nFROM seq;\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_price ON products (price); -- create an index\nALTER TABLE products DROP INDEX idx_price_rating_category; -- delete an index\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_price_rating_category ON products (created_at, price, rating, category_id); -- create an index\n\nSHOW INDEX FROM products;\n\n\n\n\nSELECT p.*, c.name FROM products p INNER JOIN categories c on p.category_id = c.id where p.id = 1;\n\nSELECT DISTINCT category FROM products;\n\nselect * from flyway_schema_history;\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_price ON products (price) ALGORITHM=INPLACE LOCK=NONE;\nALTER TABLE products DROP INDEX idx_price;\nEXPLAIN select * from products where price > 65000;",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
